<p>Promotions change prices for a while without anyone typing a discount: a cement week, a paint sale, "buy 10, get 1 free".</p>

<h2>Setting one up</h2>
<ol>
  <li>Owners and managers go to <strong>Products › Promotions</strong> and press <strong>New promotion</strong>.</li>
  <li>Choose the offer: a percentage off the shelf price, or free items when buying a quantity (buy 10, get 1 free means the 11th is free).</li>
  <li>List the products by SKU or barcode, or tick whole categories, and set the dates. Leave the branches unticked to run it everywhere.</li>
</ol>
<p>Untick <strong>Active</strong> to pause a promotion. A promotion that has been used can't be deleted; <strong>End now</strong> stops it and keeps its results.</p>

<h2>At the till</h2>
<ul>
  <li>The till applies promotions by itself. Each line shows the offer and what it took off.</li>
  <li>If two promotions cover the same product, the customer gets the one that saves them most, never both.</li>
  <li>Customers on a price list (contractors) get whichever is better: their own price or the promotion.</li>
  <li>Promotions aren't discounts, so they don't need a manager's PIN; hand discounts can still be given on top, within the usual limits.</li>
  <li>The receipt shows each saving and how much the customer saved in all.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Quotes and the online store</h2>
<p>Percentage-off promotions also price new quotes, orders and online orders (a quote keeps its price until it expires). The online store shows
  the old price struck through. Free items are only given at the till, and the offline till sells at the usual prices.</p>

<h2>How it went</h2>
<p>Each promotion's page shows how many sales used it, units sold, what it gave away and the takings on those lines.</p>
